# Tine - struck and plucked bars, tines and pans.
#
# Each note is a handful of modes tuned to the ratios of what's struck: a
# wooden bar undercut to two octaves and a third, a harder one to a twelfth,
# a metal bar left free, a tine clamped at one end, or a pan's note tuned to
# octave and twelfth.
#
# `mallet` is how hard what strikes it, `position` where (the middle keeps a
# bar's second mode quiet), `tube` the resonator under the bar, `motor` and
# `depth` the discs turning in it, `damp` the damper on a let-go note (the
# sustain pedal lifts it), `bloom` a pan's octave growing out of the note,
# `buzz` a rattle on a tine, and `roll` strikes held notes again and again.
# `pair` sounds a second copy of each note a few cents sharp, so the two
# beat as a gamelan's pairs do; it also choruses vibes and bells. Pressure
# bows a held bar.
#
# A hundred patches in eight families, plainest first:
#
#   wood     Init, marimbas, xylophones, balafons, slit and log drums (16)
#   vibes    the damper, the motor slow, fast and in time, paired, bowed (11)
#   bells    glockenspiels, tubular bells high and low, crotales (13)
#   gamelan  saron, gender, bonang and gongs, and pairs that beat (12)
#   thumb    thumb pianos with gourds and rattles, music boxes (11)
#   pans     steel pans, handpans, tongue drums, bowls struck and rubbed (15)
#   hits     blocks, claves, a low log, cowbells, agogos, triangles (13)
#   odd      bars on the wrong ratios, sour pairs, bowed glass, long metal (9)
#
# The gamelan patches are in whatever tuning the song or track has: a patch
# can't carry slendro or pelog, so set those in the tuning.
#
# Values are in each parameter's own units; `audition params Tine` lists
# them. A patch names only what it changes. Every patch sits at -22 on
# `loud` at its own note, from -24.9 (Dry Wood) to -19.0 (Bowl Rubbed): the
# short, dry ones are the quiet end, the rolled and rubbed notes the loud.
#
# Every patch but Init carries eight named macros. Destinations are
# numbered as in Tine.h's MacroDest: #1 mallet, #2 position, #3 decay,
# #4 bright, #5 damp, #6 tube, #7 motor, #8 depth, #9 bloom, #10 buzz,
# #11 roll, #12 spread, #14 volume, #15 pair. Where a macro at full moves
# the level by more than 1.5 dB, a volume route takes it back out, as far as
# the volume knob goes; where the volume is near the top the macro is
# gentler instead (Edge on the hits, Dark on the chimes). Where a family's
# macro would do nothing on a patch, another takes its place: Damper on the
# vibes left to ring, Body on the sarons and genders, Edge on the tongue
# drums and bowls. Mallet, position, decay and bright are read when a note
# is struck, and pair turns on at a note, so those macros change the next
# note.

machine Tine
role bell

# --- wood -----------------------------------------------------------------------

patch Init family=wood note=60 range=36..96

patch "Rosewood Marimba" family=wood note=60 range=36..96 role=keys
